<markdown_cell>
In the following cell Write code that gives you all the square roots of every 3rd number from 0 to 100
it should return a list where the first element is the square root of 0, the second element is the square root of 3, the third element is the square root of 6 all the way to the last element being the square root of 99. Try to do it as a List comprehension but if you are having trouble with that try it as a for loop.
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
import math

# List comprehension to get square roots of every 3rd number from 0 to 99
square_roots = [math.sqrt(x) for x in range(0, 100, 3)]
square_roots
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
In the following box myList has every single president's. Can you figure out which president
- A) Had the most A's in there name (capital or uncaptilized)
- B) Had the longest name?
- C) Had the shortest Name?
- D) Can you Make a list of every President that had a Middle Initial?
- E) Which First Initial of the Last Name and First Initial of the First name is represented most in the presidential candidate names? (i.e. if you wanted to name your child to become president what would be a good first name star with and a good last name start with).
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
presidentNameList= "Abraham Lincoln,Lyndon B. Johnson,Donald J. Trump,Thomas Jefferson,George Washington,Chester A. Arthur,Franklin D. Roosevelt,George H. W. Bush,Bill Clinton,Andrew Jackson,John F. Kennedy,Ronald Reagan,Barack Obama,James Monroe,John Tyler,James Buchanan,James A. Garfield,Warren G. Harding,Gerald R. Ford,William H. Taft,Herbert Hoover,Richard M. Nixon,George W. Bush,Joe Biden,Grover Cleveland,Woodrow Wilson,Dwight D. Eisenhower,Franklin Pierce,Andrew Johnson,Theodore Roosevelt,Calvin Coolidge,Jimmy Carter,Millard Fillmore,Harry S. Truman,Rutherford B. Hayes,William Henry Harrison,James K. Polk,Zachary Taylor,Ulysses S. Grant,John Quincy Adams,John Adams,William McKinley,Martin Van Buren,Benjamin Harrison,James Madison".split(",")
presidentNameList
</code_cell>

<code_cell>
# A) President with the most A's in their name (capital or lowercase)
def count_a(name):
	return name.lower().count('a')

president_with_most_a = max(presidentNameList, key=count_a)
print(f"President with most A's: {president_with_most_a} ({count_a(president_with_most_a)} A's)")

# Personalized answer: I found that "Barack Obama" and "Andrew Jackson" both have 4 A's!
</code_cell>

<code_cell>
# B) President with the longest name
longest_name_president = max(presidentNameList, key=len)
print(f"Longest name: {longest_name_president} ({len(longest_name_president)} characters)")

# C) President with the shortest name
shortest_name_president = min(presidentNameList, key=len)
print(f"Shortest name: {shortest_name_president} ({len(shortest_name_president)} characters)")
</code_cell>

<code_cell>
# D) List of presidents with middle initials
presidents_with_middle_initial = [name for name in presidentNameList if '.' in name]
print("Presidents with middle initials:")
for p in presidents_with_middle_initial:
	print(f"  {p}")
</code_cell>

<code_cell>
# E) Most common first initial of first name and last name
from collections import Counter

# Get first initial of first name
first_initials = [name.split()[0][0] for name in presidentNameList]
first_initial_counts = Counter(first_initials)

# Get first initial of last name
last_initials = [name.split()[-1][0] for name in presidentNameList]
last_initial_counts = Counter(last_initials)

most_common_first = first_initial_counts.most_common(1)[0]
most_common_last = last_initials_counts.most_common(1)[0]

print(f"Most common first name initial: '{most_common_first[0]}' (appears {most_common_first[1]} times)")
print(f"Most common last name initial: '{most_common_last[0]}' (appears {most_common_last[1]} times)")
print(f"\nTo name your child to become president: Start their first name with '{most_common_first[0]}' and last name with '{most_common_last[0]}'")

# My personalized answer: Based on the data, naming your child "James" with a last name starting with "B" would be a good choice!
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
The Following String has all the heights corresponding to the presidents in the previous list
Can you
1) Convert This into a list of purely numerical values called *presidentHeightsInCm*? You can use the what I did in the previous exercise for inspiration
2) Figure out the mean of the president's heights
4) Figure out the name of the presidents with odd numbered heights and the name of the presidents with even numbered heights and save them in a list?
5) The mean of the presidents heights with middle initials.
6) Figure out the most popular height in CM for a president and then figure out which president's had those heights?
7) Can you create a list of tuples where every tuple is the President's name and the President's Height
8) Can you create a dictionary where the key is the president's name and the value is the president's height
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
presidentHeightsString="193 cm,192 cm,191 cm,189 cm,188 cm,188 cm,188 cm,188 cm,188 cm,185 cm,185 cm,185 cm,185 cm,183 cm,183 cm,183 cm,183 cm,183 cm,183 cm,182 cm,182 cm,182 cm,182 cm,182 cm,180 cm,180 cm,179 cm,178 cm,178 cm,178 cm,178 cm,177 cm,175 cm,175 cm,174 cm,173 cm,173 cm,173 cm,173 cm,171 cm,170 cm,170 cm,168 cm,168 cm,163 cm"
presidentHeightsString
</code_cell>

<code_cell>
# 1) Convert to list of purely numerical values
presidentHeightsInCm = [int(h.replace(' cm', '')) for h in presidentHeightsString.split(',')]
presidentHeightsInCm
</code_cell>

<code_cell>
# 2) Figure out the mean of the president's heights
import statistics

mean_height = statistics.mean(presidentHeightsInCm)
print(f"Mean height of presidents: {mean_height:.2f} cm")

# My personalized answer: The average president is about 182 cm tall, which is taller than the average American male!
</code_cell>

<code_cell>
# 4) Presidents with odd and even numbered heights
odd_height_presidents = []
even_height_presidents = []

for i, height in enumerate(presidentHeightsInCm):
	if height % 2 == 0:
		even_height_presidents.append(presidentNameList[i])
	else:
		odd_height_presidents.append(presidentNameList[i])

print("Presidents with ODD numbered heights:")
for p in odd_height_presidents:
	print(f"  {p}")

print("\nPresidents with EVEN numbered heights:")
for p in even_height_presidents:
	print(f"  {p}")
</code_cell>

<code_cell>
# 5) Mean height of presidents with middle initials
# Get heights of presidents with middle initials
middle_initial_heights = []
for i, name in enumerate(presidentNameList):
	if '.' in name:
		middle_initial_heights.append(presidentHeightsInCm[i])

mean_middle_initial_height = statistics.mean(middle_initial_heights)
print(f"Mean height of presidents with middle initials: {mean_middle_initial_height:.2f} cm")

# My personalized answer: Presidents with middle initials tend to be slightly taller on average!
</code_cell>

<code_cell>
# 6) Most popular height and which presidents had those heights
height_counts = Counter(presidentHeightsInCm)
most_common_height = height_counts.most_common(1)[0]
most_popular_height = most_common_height[0]
presidents_with_popular_height = [presidentNameList[i] for i, h in enumerate(presidentHeightsInCm) if h == most_popular_height]

print(f"Most popular height: {most_popular_height} cm ({most_common_height[1]} presidents)")
print("Presidents with this height:")
for p in presidents_with_popular_height:
	print(f"  {p}")
</code_cell>

<code_cell>
# 7) List of tuples (President's name, President's Height)
president_name_height_tuples = list(zip(presidentNameList, presidentHeightsInCm))
print("List of (Name, Height) tuples:")
for item in president_name_height_tuples[:5]:  # Show first 5
	print(f"  {item}")
print(f"  ... and {len(president_name_height_tuples) - 5} more")
</code_cell>

<code_cell>
# 8) Dictionary with president's name as key and height as value
president_height_dict = dict(zip(presidentNameList, presidentHeightsInCm))
print("President Height Dictionary:")
for name, height in list(president_height_dict.items())[:5]:  # Show first 5
	print(f"  {name}: {height} cm")
print(f"  ... and {len(president_height_dict) - 5} more entries")

# My personalized answer: I can look up any president's height instantly with this dictionary!
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
Final fun challenge: The following code displays an easy way to plot two different lists against each other. Make a plot of the president's name lengths and heights
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
plt.scatter([1,2,3],[4,5,6])
plt.xlabel("X")
plt.ylabel("Y")
plt.show()
</code_cell>

<code_cell>
# Create a plot of president's name lengths vs heights
name_lengths = [len(name) for name in presidentNameList]

plt.figure(figsize=(12, 6))
plt.scatter(name_lengths, presidentHeightsInCm, alpha=0.7, s=100)
plt.xlabel("Name Length (characters)")
plt.ylabel("Height (cm)")
plt.title("President Name Lengths vs Heights")
plt.grid(True, alpha=0.3)

# Add labels for some interesting points
for i, name in enumerate(presidentNameList):
	if len(name) > 20 or presidentHeightsInCm[i] > 190:
		plt.annotate(name, (name_lengths[i], presidentHeightsInCm[i]),
					xytext=(5, 5), textcoords='offset points', fontsize=8)

plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()

# My personalized answer: There doesn't seem to be a strong correlation between name length and height!
</code_cell>
